<page class="methodology-page">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <h2>Testing Approach</h2>
    {{#blackBox}}
    <div class="approach-item">
        <h3>Black Box Testing</h3>
        <p>The assessor operates with no prior knowledge of the target beyond its IP addresses, URLs, or hardware identifiers — simulating an external attacker. An initial fingerprinting phase is required to discover and understand the attack surface, including open ports, DNS records, filtering equipment, and publicly exposed services. Open Source Intelligence (OSINT) is conducted to detect known vulnerabilities in identified technologies.</p>
    </div>
    {{/blackBox}}

    {{#grayBox}}
    <div class="approach-item">
        <h3>Gray Box Testing</h3>
        <p>The assessor has partial knowledge of the target environment, supplemented by information provided by the client (user accounts, documentation, etc.). This enables testing across a broader attack surface including authenticated application actions, file system access, service enumeration, and privilege escalation paths.</p>
    </div>
    {{/grayBox}}

    {{#whiteBox}}
    <div class="approach-item">
        <h3>White Box Testing</h3>
        <p>The assessor has full transparency into the system, including access to source code, architecture documentation, credentials, and configuration files. This approach enables thorough analysis of security controls, code-level vulnerabilities, and architectural design flaws.</p>
    </div>
    {{/whiteBox}}
    
